<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        WHAT THE PLAN SHEET IS: the dimming behind it, the sheet itself, the way out of it, and the
        two blocks a section can sit in. Styles only, not one value.

        IT SPLIT IN TWO ON 2026-09-07 AND THE MARKUP RATCHET IS WHAT ASKED, for the second time in
        five days and the second time pointing at the same seam. A failure that can be escalated
        needed a template of its own, and this file stood at 445 lines against a ceiling of 376 -
        so what one LINE of a plan looks like went to PlanLines.xaml and what the SHEET looks like
        stayed here. That is the division this file's first version already described in its own
        opening sentence, made real once it had to be.

        NEITHER HALF LOADS ON ITS OWN, which is what the guard's own message warns about: both use
        names from Colours.xaml and Values.xaml through StaticResource, resolved WHILE THE FILE IS
        READ. So the order in App.xaml is the behaviour, and PlanLines.xaml is merged immediately
        before this one. Four places repeat that order and all four had to learn the new name:
        App.xaml, WpfHost in the tests, tools/wpfui-probe, and the pool in AppearanceGuards.

        MERGED AFTER Controls.xaml, which is not optional. The close button below reaches
        PrimaryActionFocusRing through StaticResource, and a dictionary merged after this one is a
        name that does not exist yet.

        Nothing here binds a Click - `docs/10` and AppearanceGuards both record why, and it is not
        a style choice: a resource dictionary has no code-behind, so a handler named in markup
        cannot be resolved from one. The close button is the borderline case and it passes, because
        a style carries no handler - PlanView.xaml still owns the Click and this hands it the look.
    -->

    <!--
        THE WAY OUT OF THE PANEL, AND IT BECAME A MARK RATHER THAN A WORD ON 2026-09-02 FOR ONE
        MEASURED REASON: it was standing on the title's line and taking the room the title needed.

        Read off the owner's capture of the built window. The panel is 380 wide, its padding takes
        28 and the title's own group margin 20, which leaves 332 - and the library's Close button
        was about 66 of it. The title left with 266 could not fit "AssignedAccessManager" beside
        the words around it, so a two-line title became a THREE-line title, a hundred pixels of a
        panel 380 tall. Giving those 66 back is the whole of this change: the cross is about 28
        across, so the title gets roughly 38 points and its third line back.

        AND IT STOPS COMPETING, WHICH IS THE SECOND HALF. A filled, bordered rectangle carrying a
        word was the heaviest object in the panel after the title, sitting beside it. What closes a
        panel is furniture rather than an action - the actions here are the button naming the act and
        Interrupt - so it wears no fill until a pointer is on it.

        THE MARK IS A Path AND NOT A CHARACTER, the same decision as the disclosure in Chips.xaml
        and for the same two measured reasons: a glyph needs a font that carries it on Windows
        Server, and a TextBlock in CONTENT gets an automation node of its own. A Shape gets no
        peer, so the tree keeps one node for one button - and PlanView.xaml sets
        AutomationProperties.Name on it, because a button with no text has no name without one.

        THE RING IS THE PRIMARY ACTION'S, BORROWED BY NAME. It is a rounded rectangle the size of
        whatever it is drawn on, so nothing about it is specific to that button - and `ADR-23`
        prefers one ring named once to a second one that will drift from it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanClose" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{StaticResource PrimaryActionFocusRing}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <!--
                        Transparent rather than no brush at all, which is `docs/10` trap 11: a
                        transparent brush TAKES the pointer and a missing one does not, so without
                        this the button would only be pressable on the two strokes of the cross.
                    -->
                    <Border x:Name="Face"
                            Background="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <Path Data="M 0,0 L 10,10 M 10,0 L 0,10"
                              Stroke="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                              StrokeThickness="1.3"
                              StrokeStartLineCap="Round"
                              StrokeEndLineCap="Round" />
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <!--
                            THE PANEL'S HOVER SINCE 2026-09-16: the window's read 1.30 on this sheet,
                            so pointing at the close mark showed nothing. Argument beside the brush.
                        -->
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHoverOnPanel}" />
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        A BLOCK WITH A LINE DOWN ITS LEFT SIDE, WHICH IS THE ONE NEW IDEA IN THE PANEL'S REDESIGN.

        NAMED PanelBlock SINCE 2026-09-16, PlanBlock BEFORE, because a second panel took it that day:
        the details panel draws each of its four sections as this block (Themes/Details.xaml,
        docs/PROJEKT-SZCZEGOLY-20260916.md). A style called Plan-something inside the panel about one
        entry would be a name read literally by whoever comes next, and GUI rule 5 wants a change to
        the block to be a change in one place - which it only is while both screens name the same
        style. It still lives in this file because this file is merged before Details.xaml and the
        plan sheet was its first home.

        WHAT WAS WRONG. Every section of the panel was a heading with sentences under it and nothing
        else - six of them in a column, told apart only by their words. The owner's capture shows the
        result: three labels of equal weight down a narrow column, and no way to see which block is
        WHAT WILL HAPPEN and which is a note about it. There was nothing to look at, only things to
        read.

        WHAT THE RULE SAYS AND WHY IT IS CHEAP. A block that matters gets a line beside it and the
        COLOUR OF THE LINE SAYS WHICH KIND. No fill, so nothing new has to clear a contrast ratio
        against the panel, and no corner, no shadow, no second surface. Two blocks carry one, which
        is the whole list on purpose: the steps, because they are what the machine would do, and the
        line saying why the button underneath will refuse. A rule on all six would be furniture
        again.

        NOT ONE NEW VALUE, AND THAT IS AN ARGUMENT RATHER THAN THRIFT. The rule is BorderPanelEdge,
        the one-point left edge this panel already draws against the list, so every hairline on this
        screen is the same weight - a three-point accent rule would be the only stroke of its
        thickness in the window and would read as a different product.

        THE PADDING WAS PaddingPanel FOR ONE DAY AND IT COST TOO MUCH. That value is 14,12,14,12,
        which with MarginSection's 18 and 4 is 46 points of vertical chrome per block - on a panel
        355 points tall, for two blocks, that is a quarter of it spent on air. Measured on the
        owner's own capture the day after: with the refusal block on screen the body was 24 per cent
        of the panel and the footer was 44. PadCell keeps the eight points of left air the rule
        needs and spends four above and below instead of twelve.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PanelBlock" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceChipEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderPanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginSection}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE STEPS BLOCK GOES WITH ITS STEPS, SINCE 2026-09-23 - UX-GUI-003. A plan made only of
        refusals opens this sheet, and it opened on "In this order" over nothing with the reason
        under it. A trigger here rather than a property on Planned, which stands at the ceiling of
        methods for a type, and rather than a Visibility in PlanView, which stands at the ceiling of
        markup - Steps is already raised with every plan.

        STEPS.COUNT IS NOT A PUBLIC PROPERTY OF WHAT STEPS HOLDS, AND THE TRIGGER FIRES ANYWAY.
        Measured 2026-09-23: the collection expression makes a compiler type (and an array when
        empty), and reflection finds no public Count on either. WPF still resolves the path, and
        RefusedPlanTests reads this block's real Visibility both ways, so the guard is the test.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanStepsBlock" TargetType="Border" BasedOn="{StaticResource PanelBlock}">
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Steps.Count}" Value="0">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The refusal sits in the FOOTER rather than among the sections, so it takes the footer's
        rhythm rather than the section rhythm: a gap under it and none above, because the rule the
        footer already draws is what separates it from the body.
    -->
    <!--
        THE RULE WENT FROM WARNING TO REFUSED ON 2026-09-02, ON THE OWNER'S WORDS. The sentence
        this block was made for says this session has no administrator rights - Planned writes
        nothing else into Blocked - and amber says "something might go wrong" where the truth is
        "nothing here can be done at all". The rule and the words inside it agree.

        TWO KINDS OF SENTENCE SINCE 2026-09-16, ONE LOOK. The footer also stands the reason for a
        heavy confirmation in this block - the machine would go down, or the process ends more than
        the entry named. "Nothing can be done" and "this can be done and takes the machine with it"
        are both "stop and read this", and a third block in the same red would be a second name
        for one look (ADR-23). What tells the two apart is the text: ProblemText explains a grey
        button, DangerText warns over a live one.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PanelBlockRefused" TargetType="Border" BasedOn="{StaticResource PanelBlock}">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE DIMMING BEHIND THE SHEET, 2026-09-02. It fills whatever it is put in and it EATS THE
        CLICKS, which is the half that is not decoration: while this question is on screen the list
        under it must not be selectable, or somebody answers a plan about a row they have since
        changed. A brush is what makes that true - `docs/10` records that a transparent fill takes
        hit tests and NO fill takes none, so the colour here is load bearing twice over.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanScrim" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceScrim}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE SHEET ITSELF, AND IT REPLACED A SIDE PANEL ON 2026-09-02 ON THE OWNER'S DECISION.

        WHAT THE PANEL DID WRONG WAS NOT ITS LOOK, IT WAS ITS PLACE. Sharing a row with the list
        meant taking width from it, so opening a plan trimmed `Display name` to nothing, cut the
        account column in half and grew a horizontal scrollbar under a list that never needed one.
        At 1000 by 800 it was worse: the panel took its own vertical scrollbar and a section heading
        was left on screen with nothing under it. Every one of those is a width argument, and a
        sheet does not have that argument - it is centred over the window and takes what it needs.

        A PLAN IS A QUESTION, NOT AN INSPECTOR, and that is the reason underneath the geometry. It
        asks whether to change this machine, it is answered once, and until it is answered nothing
        else in the window is worth pressing. A panel says "look at this beside your list". This
        says "answer this". The cost is real and was said out loud before it was chosen: the list
        can no longer be read while the plan is open.

        BORDER, RADIUS AND SHADOW TOGETHER, because two of the three read as a mistake. A fill with
        no edge is a rectangle somebody painted over the window - which is what the panel looked
        like and what the owner called flat. The shadow is what says WHICH layer is on top, and
        without it a centred box just covers things.

        THE MARGIN IS THE MAXIMUM HEIGHT. Centred with air above and below, the sheet can never be
        taller than the window minus that air, and its body scrolls inside itself instead - so the
        buttons at the foot are always on screen. That is the fault this replaces, said as geometry.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanSheet" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanel}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource CornerPlanSheet}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PaddingPanel}" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginPlanSheet}" />

        <Setter Property="Effect">
            <Setter.Value>
                <!--
                    Black rather than a colour, and soft rather than tight: a shadow that can be
                    SEEN as a shape reads as a border drawn badly. This one only has to say that
                    there is space between the sheet and the window behind it.
                -->
                <DropShadowEffect Color="Black"
                                  BlurRadius="28"
                                  ShadowDepth="8"
                                  Direction="270"
                                  Opacity="0.55" />
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE WORD ON THE BUTTON AT THE FOOT OF THE SHEET, TRIMMED RATHER THAN CUT, since 2026-09-15 -
        the day the button started naming the entry it acts on. "Stop Windows Search" is short, and
        the longest display name on this machine is 116 characters of translated Windows - measured
        2026-09-15, beside WidthCarryOutMost. The view caps the button there, and a capped button
        with a plain string clips its word at the edge, mid-letter - `docs/11` 3.5 says an ellipsis
        or nothing. A string reaches a ContentPresenter as a TextBlock nobody can reach, so the
        trimming has to come in through a template for the content.

        A DataTemplate AND NOT A ControlTemplate, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THIS IS SAFE. `docs/10`
        trap 16 records that a TextBlock put into a ControlTemplate loses its automation node and
        sixteen texts vanished from the tree the last time this product tried it. Measured
        2026-09-15 before this was written, on a laid-out button: content through this template
        keeps exactly what a plain string has - one TextBlock child under the button's peer and the
        same Name - and MaxWidth on the button reaches the text through it. The button's name is
        bound explicitly in the view as well, so a probe reading planCarryOut never depends on
        which of the two mechanisms WPF chooses.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="TrimmedButtonLabel">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        THE BOX OF SECONDS, WEARING THE PROBLEM COLOUR ON ITS EDGE WHILE IT SAYS SOMETHING THAT IS
        NOT SECONDS - 2026-09-15, point 5 of `docs/11` 2.14. GUI rule 3: a field has an error state,
        and this is the only field on the sheet that can be wrong without being empty.

        THE MECHANISM MOVED TO ReportingBox IN Controls.xaml ON 2026-09-23, when the search box
        became the second field to need it - GUI rule 7. The argument for each part of it went with
        it: the trigger on Validation.HasError, the error adorner drawn above the library's own
        bottom line (the owner's remark of 2026-09-16 about a red edge on three sides and none along
        the bottom), and the BorderBrush setter left for a render with no adorner layer.

        THE KEY STAYS, AS A STYLE ON THAT ONE, so the sheet keeps naming what the box IS rather than
        how it is drawn, and so the day the box of seconds needs something the search box does not,
        it has a place to go. Controls.xaml is merged before this file, which is what lets
        StaticResource find the base while this one is read - App.xaml.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="WaitingBox" TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource ReportingBox}" />
</ResourceDictionary>
